WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:03.690
- [Narrator] Corporate giants
like Amazon, United Health

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.499
and CVS are investing billions

00:00:05.499 --> 00:00:08.010
into primary care practices,

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:09.697
with deals tapping into the nation's

00:00:09.697 --> 00:00:11.879
$4 trillion healthcare market.

00:00:11.879 --> 00:00:13.049
And it's part of a shift

00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:15.420
in America's costly healthcare system

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.790
moving away from the
traditional Fee-For-Service,

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.530
towards Value-Based care.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:21.420
That model is about paying providers

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.130
for keeping patients healthy,

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.770
while a Fee-For-Service model
is when medical providers

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:28.438
are paid based on the amount
of services performed.

00:00:28.438 --> 00:00:30.608
So why are companies spending billions

00:00:30.608 --> 00:00:32.730
on primary care practices,

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:35.400
and what could the growing
shift to Value-Based care

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.073
mean for doctors, patients
and health insurers?

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.100
The US spends more per
capita on healthcare

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.870
than any other developed nation,

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:46.170
but still fares worse on
most major health measures.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.180
Health experts say the incentives baked

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.100
into America's Fee-For-Service system

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:51.840
are part of the problem.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.010
- So critics say that under
the Fee-For-Service model,

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.770
it basically encourages
providers to recommend

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.620
as many services as possible,
which drives up the cost

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.471
of healthcare without necessarily ensuring

00:01:03.471 --> 00:01:06.603
patients are at the forefront of care.

00:01:06.603 --> 00:01:08.730
- [Narrator] Essentially,
doctors are rewarded

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:11.145
for the volume of healthcare
services they provide.

00:01:11.145 --> 00:01:14.670
Now, companies like
Amazon, CVS, and Walgreens

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.319
are tapping into the growing
Value-Based care movement.

00:01:17.319 --> 00:01:20.730
That model provides incentives
to improve patient health

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:23.329
outcomes within hospitals
and doctor's offices.

00:01:23.329 --> 00:01:26.250
- In Value-Based care,
the idea is to keep track

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:27.900
of the patient, to have more contact

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.208
between primary care doctors and patients,

00:01:30.208 --> 00:01:32.580
and reduce the amount of time that people

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.910
actually spend in hospitals
where the costs are very high.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:37.710
- [Narrator] Those costs
are high for patients

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.030
and health insurers.

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.129
Some private health plans
and government programs

00:01:41.129 --> 00:01:43.950
like Medicare have already
adopted this model.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:45.432
They want to move more
of the healthcare system

00:01:45.432 --> 00:01:47.891
to that approach, which
makes providers accountable

00:01:47.891 --> 00:01:51.270
for some or all of the cost
of their patient's care.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.910
While Value-Based care can
be traced back decades,

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.560
the 2010 Affordable Care Act

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.440
accelerated the government's
transition to this model.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.390
- The government essentially said that

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.740
it'll start shifting a lot of healthcare

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:04.590
towards Value-Based care

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.600
with the idea being to control costs.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.998
- [Narrator] Still, there can be downsides

00:02:07.998 --> 00:02:09.660
to a Value-Based model.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.665
- [David] A key concern is
that patients might be delayed

00:02:11.665 --> 00:02:15.630
in seeing specialists or
in undergoing procedures,

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:18.060
and basically insurers
and providers could be

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.883
doing that to prevent
costs from escalating.

00:02:20.883 --> 00:02:22.514
- [Narrator] One obstacle behind shifting

00:02:22.514 --> 00:02:24.720
to a Value-Based model is a shortage

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.140
in primary care physicians or PCPs.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.937
Because the US healthcare system rewards

00:02:28.937 --> 00:02:32.310
more expensive procedures,
medical students may prefer

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.273
to become cardiologists or surgeons.

00:02:34.273 --> 00:02:37.710
- [David] The cardiologist
makes close to $500,000

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:39.817
on average, whereas a
primary care physician

00:02:39.817 --> 00:02:42.072
will make around 200,000.

00:02:42.072 --> 00:02:43.115
- [Narrator] In other countries,

00:02:43.115 --> 00:02:45.450
PCPs compose a much higher proportion

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.400
of all practicing physicians.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.560
For example, 45% in France,

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.746
compared to 12% in the United States.

00:02:51.746 --> 00:02:54.920
Yet PCPs play a central role
in keeping people healthy

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.750
and preventing problems before they begin.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.003
- [David] What happens is many Americans

00:02:59.003 --> 00:03:00.755
don't see a primary care physician.

00:03:00.755 --> 00:03:03.095
They can't find one, or they
can't get an appointment

00:03:03.095 --> 00:03:04.140
for a month.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:05.805
Many times people don't
know they have something,

00:03:05.805 --> 00:03:07.470
and they end up in a hospital

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:09.850
where they have to get a much
more expensive procedure.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:12.720
- [Narrator] To help reduce
higher health costs downstream,

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.643
a host of companies are
now spending billions

00:03:14.643 --> 00:03:16.479
on primary care practices.

00:03:16.479 --> 00:03:18.980
UnitedHealth, a massive
conglomerate that includes

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:21.653
60,000 physicians and
an insurance business,

00:03:21.653 --> 00:03:24.614
is one of the furthest
along in PCP investments.

00:03:24.614 --> 00:03:27.198
Through its medical
provider arm Optum Care,

00:03:27.198 --> 00:03:30.480
it has been buying up
multi-specialty physician practices

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.495
to provide care at home,
virtually and on site.

00:03:33.495 --> 00:03:36.167
The idea is to keep people
out of the hospital,

00:03:36.167 --> 00:03:38.713
because that's where
the costs are highest.

00:03:38.713 --> 00:03:42.300
Meanwhile, Amazon is planning
to buy primary care company

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:45.390
One Medical, a practice
that operates more than 180

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:47.803
medical offices in 25 US markets.

00:03:47.803 --> 00:03:51.095
The $3.9 billion deal could give Amazon

00:03:51.095 --> 00:03:53.070
a larger foothold in offering

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:55.102
healthcare services to employers.

00:03:55.102 --> 00:03:58.320
The company CVS Health is
also stepping into the mix

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.590
with a deal to pay $8
billion for Signify Health,

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.470
a company focused on in-home evaluations.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:08.074
- Ultimately, CVS wants to
be not only your pharmacist

00:04:08.074 --> 00:04:10.620
and your insurer, through its AETNA arm,

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:13.077
but it also wants to
be your village clinic.

00:04:13.077 --> 00:04:14.607
- [Narrator] Then there's Walgreens.

00:04:14.607 --> 00:04:17.790
After buying a controlling
stake in startup clinic chain

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.880
VillageMD, it struck a
roughly $9 billion deal

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.800
to combine with Summit Health,

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.134
a large owner of urgent care centers.

00:04:25.134 --> 00:04:27.804
So how exactly do these
companies plan to make money

00:04:27.804 --> 00:04:29.426
off of these investments?

00:04:29.426 --> 00:04:31.887
In practice, a Value-Based
reimbursement model

00:04:31.887 --> 00:04:34.050
means providing financial rewards

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:35.640
for better patient outcomes.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.070
It can also be tied to
a capitation system,

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.380
which pays healthcare
providers a fixed amount

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:43.620
for each patient they deliver
care to per unit of time.

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:47.250
This prioritizes the patient
and cost saving over services,

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:49.658
which can significantly
reduce health spending.

00:04:49.658 --> 00:04:51.951
Still, some healthcare economists worry

00:04:51.951 --> 00:04:53.541
whether this Value-Based approach

00:04:53.541 --> 00:04:56.039
will benefit patients or just reduce costs

00:04:56.039 --> 00:04:59.460
and improve the bottom line
for providers and insurers.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:01.293
- For example, a primary care physician

00:05:01.293 --> 00:05:04.295
might be more reluctant to allow a patient

00:05:04.295 --> 00:05:05.632
to go see a specialist

00:05:05.632 --> 00:05:09.540
or to perform a more expensive procedure.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.490
- [Narrator] Ultimately,
some healthcare economists

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:14.130
say moving away from a
Fee-For-Service model

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:17.520
could help temper ballooning
healthcare costs in the US.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:18.976
- [David] As more and more
of these bigger companies

00:05:18.976 --> 00:05:21.180
get into Value-Based care,

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:22.980
it's just going to accelerate the move

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:24.810
towards Value-Based care and away

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.904
from this Fee-For-Service model.

00:05:26.904 --> 00:05:30.237
(bright cheerful music)

